			],
			"Message": "refactor(Mountain): Modularize IPC health and process command units\n\nRestructure the `Source/Binary/IPC` subsystem to align with CodeEditorLand's distributed architecture and PascalCase naming standards. The monolithic `HealthCommand.rs` and `ProcessCommand.rs` files have been decomposed into flat module trees where each Tauri command resides in its own unit file.\n\nFor `HealthCommand`, extract logic into dedicated modules (`cocoon_extension_host_health`, `shared_process_service_health`, etc.) to serve as thin probes for Wind's `SharedProcessProxy`. For `ProcessCommand`, split the polyfill bridge into individual units (`process_get_exec_path`, `process_get_memory_info`, etc.) to maintain snake_case wire names for Node.js compatibility while enforcing the \"one export per file\" pattern internally.\n\nThis reorganization reduces file complexity, clarifies the boundary between Mountain's Rust backend and Wind's TypeScript frontend, and prepares the IPC layer for finer-grained testing and type safety.",
			"SHA": "e6c40574156a55c0aea7c472481573328e1b4252"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:46:24Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1251,
				1
			],
			"Message": "fix(Mountain): Disable app sandbox and enable hardened runtime exemptions\n\nConfigure Entitlements.plist to disable the macOS App Sandbox, which is incompatible with a code editor's requirement to access arbitrary file system locations selected by the user. With sandbox enabled, the editor cannot open folders outside its container, breaking standard workflows like 'Open Recent' and drag-and-drop.\n\nSimultaneously, enable specific Hardened Runtime exemptions required by the embedded Node.js sidecar (Cocoon) and Tauri dependencies:\n- `allow-jit` and `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`: Essential for V8 JIT compilation in the extension host.\n- `allow-dyld-environment-variables`: Required for extension hosts spawning helper binaries with custom linker paths.\n- `disable-library-validation`: Necessary to load unsigned native addons (e.g., sharp, esbuild) bundled via npm.\n- `apple-events`: Permits extensions to invoke external system commands via AppleScript (e.g., 'Reveal in Finder').\n\nThis ensures the binary can be signed and notarized while maintaining full functionality for the editor and its extension ecosystem.",
			"SHA": "8d0dd90346bc8ae1bea2143c155fd332e5abb547"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:40:48Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1251,
				2
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Mountain): Modularize Environment providers into distributed units\n\nAlign the `Source/Environment` subsystem with CodeEditorLand's distributed architecture and PascalCase naming standards. Previously monolithic files (`TestProvider.rs`, `WebviewProvider.rs`) have been restructured into flat modules where each concept resides in its own unit file.\n\nFor `TestProvider`: Extract `TestControllerState::Struct`, `TestRunStatus::Enum`, `TestResult::Struct`, `TestRun::Struct`, and `TestProviderState::Struct` into dedicated files (`TestControllerState`, `TestRunStatus`, etc.). Refactor the `TestController` trait implementation to use these new types (e.g., `TestRunStatus::Enum::Passed`) and simplify the parent file to handle only logic dispatch.\n\nFor `WebviewProvider`: Extract `WebviewLifecycleState::Enum` and `WebviewMessage::Struct` into `WebviewLifecycleState` and `WebviewMessage` modules. Move implementation details for `Lifecycle`, `Configuration`, and `Messaging` into private submodules accessed via `#[path]` directives, exposing only the necessary DTOs and the main `impl` block in the root file.\n\nThis reorganization reduces file size, enforces the \"one export per file\" pattern, and prepares the environment layer for finer-grained IPC integration and type safety.\n\nRefs #Telemetry",
			"SHA": "4cd523aea02e5f592326b6412600b2645691656c"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:40:17Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1251,
				2
			],
			"Message": "fix(Mountain): Enhance AppLifecycle resolution and inject shell environment on boot\n\nThe `AppLifecycle` module previously relied solely on Tauri's `resource_dir` for resolving the `Sky/Target` path. This caused raw release binary launches (outside the `.app` bundle) to fail resolving the frontend root, resulting in 404s for extension-contributed icons (e.g., GitLens, Roo) and empty view containers.\n\nRefactor `AppLifecycleSetup` to implement a multi-tier fallback strategy: first check the standard `.app` bundle layout (`Contents/Resources`), then the monorepo relative path (`../../../Sky/Target`), and finally an executable-relative fallback. This ensures the `STATIC_APPLICATION_ROOT` is correctly set regardless of the launch context.\n\nAdditionally, inject the user's interactive shell environment (loading `PATH`, `NVM_DIR`, `HOMEBREW_PREFIX`) in `Entry` before spawning child processes. This resolves issues where GUI-launched applications (Finder/Dock) lack necessary CLI tools (Homebrew, Git, Node/NVM), causing extension host failures. Update command handler registrations to strictly adhere to PascalCase naming conventions (e.g., `Initialize::Fn`, `MountainProvideHover::MountainProvideHover`).\n\nImpact: Fixes icon rendering in production builds, ensures extension host stability in GUI launches, and standardizes binary entry point naming.",
			"SHA": "72f7a764914ca9ec250a6f3a3da8481723b892cd"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:39:33Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1251,
				4
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Expand extension root discovery and modularize telemetry/hover units\n\nExtend the LocalhostPlugin to resolve built-in extensions from multiple distribution paths (`Contents/Resources/extensions`, `Contents/Resources/app/extensions`) and the development monorepo layout. This ensures icon stylesheets for the 93 bundled extensions render correctly in packaged `.app` builds and prevents empty boxes in the extension view.\n\nApply the established distributed architecture pattern to the PostHog analytics and Hover command subsystems. Refactor `PostHogPlugin` and `Command/Hover` into distinct unit files (e.g., `Initialize::Fn`, `Position::Struct`, `HoverContent::Enum`), enforcing PascalCase naming and separating DTO definitions from logic. This aligns with recent telemetry refactors and prepares the codebase for stricter IPC type safety.\n\nImpact: Fixes extension icon rendering in production builds and standardizes the module structure for observability and LSP features.",
			"SHA": "0bb57d42016d8ab3ea6bc79312fdbcb8681e2657"
		}
	],
